// random.trt: the random library's functions written in Turtle, pick and
// chance, on the parts written in Go (the "random" sentence, seed,
// shuffle and sample). It's built into turtle; "import random" loads it.
// items_of is a helper, private to it.
//
// shuffle and sample go through every item, which is far faster in Go
// (a Turtle shuffle of 1,000 items took 700µs, Go's 5µs), so they stay
// there. pick and chance do a little work around one random value, which
// Turtle does quickly (about 2µs and 0.25µs a call).

// items_of is what pick chooses from: a list's or set's items, a map's
// keys, a string's characters.
def items_of[x, fn]
    safe
        return change x to list
    handle [] e .
        fail "{fn} needs a list, set, map or string, got {x}"
    safe [end]
def [end]

def pick[x]
    items = items_of[x, "pick"]
    if ] length of items == 0 [
        fail "pick: {x} is empty, so there's nothing to pick"
    if [end]
    return items at get[random integer from 0 to length of items - 1]
def [end]

def chance[p]
    if ] p < 0 || p > 1 [
        fail "chance: needs a number from 0 to 1 (0.3 is 30% of the time), got {p}"
    if [end]
    return random float < p
def [end]
